WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.334 --> 00:00:08.630
Now, before I had the honor
as a playwright to make it to Broadway,

00:00:08.672 --> 00:00:11.967
which also opened the door for me
to work in TV and film,

00:00:12.008 --> 00:00:14.427
like everybody, I had a dream growing up.

00:00:15.053 --> 00:00:17.097
Now, before I tell you
what that dream was,

00:00:17.138 --> 00:00:18.765
you have to promise not to laugh.

00:00:18.807 --> 00:00:19.808
(Laughter)

00:00:19.849 --> 00:00:21.560
You're going to laugh? Promise?

00:00:22.227 --> 00:00:23.478
Alright.

00:00:24.062 --> 00:00:25.814
When I was growing up,

00:00:25.814 --> 00:00:27.065
I wanted to be ...

00:00:27.649 --> 00:00:29.067
a superhero.

00:00:29.734 --> 00:00:31.945
See, I wanted to be
what I saw so vibrantly

00:00:31.945 --> 00:00:35.365
on the pages of my Marvel
and DC comic books.

00:00:35.365 --> 00:00:39.119
I actually used to sit on a stoop
in the projects with my godbrother Johnny,

00:00:39.119 --> 00:00:42.956
and we would create our own stories,
writing and drawing them all.

00:00:42.998 --> 00:00:46.459
Now I have to confess,
years before Miles Morales,

00:00:46.501 --> 00:00:48.878
we actually made
the first Black Spider-Man.

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:50.797
(Laughter)

00:00:50.839 --> 00:00:53.550
Two of them. What can I say?
We was from Queens.

00:00:53.592 --> 00:00:54.634
(Laughter)

00:00:54.676 --> 00:00:59.472
Now, as we know, there's always
a moment in a superhero's life

00:00:59.514 --> 00:01:02.434
where they realize
that they have superpowers.

00:01:02.475 --> 00:01:04.477
Now mine was revealed to me

00:01:04.519 --> 00:01:08.148
by my arch nemesis,
my eighth grade English teacher --

00:01:08.148 --> 00:01:09.733
(Laughter)

00:01:10.442 --> 00:01:13.778
after he gave the class
an assignment to write a poem.

00:01:13.778 --> 00:01:15.322
What happened after I did that

00:01:15.322 --> 00:01:17.657
would forever change
the course of my life.

00:01:17.657 --> 00:01:20.702
Because when I turned in that assignment,
the poem was so good,

00:01:20.702 --> 00:01:23.204
my teacher actually thought
I plagiarized it.

00:01:23.204 --> 00:01:26.708
He asked me how did I know
what all these literary devices were

00:01:26.750 --> 00:01:29.377
that I didn't even know
had names at the time.

00:01:29.419 --> 00:01:31.880
Because up until this point,
because of my dyslexia,

00:01:31.921 --> 00:01:34.090
English and writing
was always my kryptonite.

00:01:34.716 --> 00:01:39.054
I didn't even realize that all those years
of consuming hip-hop music and culture

00:01:39.095 --> 00:01:42.223
was actually teaching me
how to use literary techniques.

00:01:42.265 --> 00:01:46.561
Like when Tupac said, "Picture perfect,
I paint a perfect picture,"

00:01:47.646 --> 00:01:49.731
I would find out
that that was alliteration.

00:01:51.107 --> 00:01:52.400
Hmm.

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:53.652
I wanted to try that.

00:01:54.736 --> 00:01:59.074
"Pompous politicians pressure people
to push towards their God."

00:02:00.158 --> 00:02:01.451
Hmm.

00:02:01.451 --> 00:02:04.537
Now, right in that moment,
a new power was unlocked.

00:02:05.705 --> 00:02:09.376
"We stood by street signs that were
slightly slanted in the slums of the city.

00:02:09.376 --> 00:02:12.921
Then we gradually, geographically
graphed the graffiti on granite walls.

00:02:12.962 --> 00:02:16.383
And this was driven from the rage
of the reaction of racism that was --"

00:02:16.424 --> 00:02:17.842
Alright. Y'all get the point.

00:02:17.884 --> 00:02:19.260
(Applause)

00:02:19.302 --> 00:02:20.553
Thank you.

00:02:23.181 --> 00:02:25.558
I would soon find out what a metaphor was.

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:30.689
A simile, double entendre, onomatopoeia,
end rhyme, internal rhyme and so on.

00:02:30.689 --> 00:02:32.399
And I felt myself getting stronger

00:02:32.399 --> 00:02:34.943
with every word and phrase
I was putting together.

00:02:36.027 --> 00:02:38.196
Now a couple of years later,
in high school,

00:02:38.196 --> 00:02:40.615
I would discover the world of slam poetry.

00:02:41.282 --> 00:02:43.785
I had just recently got cut
from my basketball team,

00:02:43.785 --> 00:02:47.664
so I had a lot of extra time on my hands
that I wasn't used to.

00:02:47.706 --> 00:02:51.876
So what I did was
I transformed my competitive energy

00:02:51.918 --> 00:02:54.713
from the court to the stage.

00:02:54.754 --> 00:02:57.424
Now this was years before
I ended up going to college

00:02:57.465 --> 00:03:02.429
and studying Chekhov and Meisner
and William Shakespeare and August Wilson.

00:03:02.470 --> 00:03:05.640
Back then I was studying
underground battle rap kings

00:03:05.682 --> 00:03:08.226
like Loaded Lux and Mook,

00:03:08.268 --> 00:03:11.354
and prophets that called
themselves Def Poets.

00:03:12.063 --> 00:03:13.314
See, those men,

00:03:14.399 --> 00:03:16.025
they looked and sounded like me.

00:03:17.193 --> 00:03:19.946
They sounded like
the neighborhoods I lived in.

00:03:19.946 --> 00:03:21.740
They sounded like home.

00:03:22.282 --> 00:03:26.828
So now, like those poets,
Lemon Andersen and Black Ice,

00:03:26.828 --> 00:03:29.330
I started to look at people different.

00:03:29.372 --> 00:03:32.167
Stories started coming
in and out of my mind

00:03:32.208 --> 00:03:35.211
just from observing
the everyday world around me.

00:03:35.795 --> 00:03:39.257
Now I can make the regular, most mundane

00:03:39.299 --> 00:03:43.344
blue collar worker of the middle class
sound like a superhero.

00:03:43.887 --> 00:03:46.514
Like I remember there was always
this guy on the corner

00:03:46.556 --> 00:03:48.141
telling stories and reminiscing.

00:03:48.183 --> 00:03:49.559
We all know this guy, right?

00:03:49.601 --> 00:03:52.854
And all his stories would start off with,
"Y'all, I used to be nice."

00:03:52.896 --> 00:03:54.397
(Laughter)

00:03:54.397 --> 00:03:56.357
Most people would write him off.

00:03:56.357 --> 00:03:59.903
But me, I saw more than a person
whose dream was deferred.

00:04:00.695 --> 00:04:04.824
I saw a person who came alive
in those stories.

00:04:04.824 --> 00:04:07.118
I knew that he was more than those dreams.

00:04:07.911 --> 00:04:09.287
Whether they knew it ...

00:04:09.996 --> 00:04:11.247
or not.

00:04:14.876 --> 00:04:16.461
I used to be nice, yo.

00:04:17.629 --> 00:04:21.132
Nice like Jordan fadeaway nice.

00:04:21.174 --> 00:04:22.550
When I stepped on the court,

00:04:22.592 --> 00:04:24.803
stars would align,
the world stopped spinning,

00:04:24.844 --> 00:04:27.222
gravity would cease
and I appeared to jump higher.

00:04:27.931 --> 00:04:29.390
You see, the angle of my form

00:04:29.432 --> 00:04:32.644
was nothing less than Da Vinci's
sketch of the ideal man.

00:04:33.228 --> 00:04:36.147
Man, I used to be nice.

00:04:36.147 --> 00:04:37.982
I wasn't fast, but I was quick.

00:04:37.982 --> 00:04:41.277
My defense was well equipped
and my assists were crisp.

00:04:41.277 --> 00:04:44.697
Since birth I was chosen,
I used to part defenses like Moses.

00:04:44.697 --> 00:04:46.407
Due to my vision, I could envision

00:04:46.407 --> 00:04:48.660
a player's position
before they was positioned.

00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:49.953
I was conditioned.

00:04:49.953 --> 00:04:53.122
My no-looks were textbook,
and scouts even said I was off the hook.

00:04:53.164 --> 00:04:57.001
Look, my crossover would put my opponent
on pause and cause the crowd to go,

00:04:57.043 --> 00:04:59.462
Oh!

00:04:59.504 --> 00:05:01.506
Yeah, I used to be nice.

00:05:01.548 --> 00:05:04.926
I used to pin shots on the backboard,
never bored, and when I scored,

00:05:04.968 --> 00:05:07.428
I transformed into something
that wasn't even human.

00:05:07.470 --> 00:05:10.431
But who knew I would be ruined
by a guy so envious of my game,

00:05:10.473 --> 00:05:11.808
his aim was me.

00:05:11.850 --> 00:05:13.768
So when I heard the snap of my knee ...

00:05:17.438 --> 00:05:18.815
everything went silent.

00:05:21.401 --> 00:05:23.653
I no longer heard the crowd or my team.

00:05:25.864 --> 00:05:27.740
My years of dedication

00:05:28.908 --> 00:05:30.994
deteriorated with my dream.

00:05:31.703 --> 00:05:34.664
So I lay there on the floor,
four minutes left, fourth quarter.

00:05:34.706 --> 00:05:38.334
No water, just a jersey drenched in sweat.
Regrets of trying to dunk on him.

00:05:39.085 --> 00:05:41.379
No need to ask why.
This is the hand I was dealt.

00:05:41.421 --> 00:05:42.881
No sleeves. I couldn't cheat.

00:05:42.922 --> 00:05:44.215
Defeat didn't taste good.

00:05:46.134 --> 00:05:47.594
But I used to be nice, yo.

00:05:48.386 --> 00:05:51.139
Nice like Jordan fadeaway nice.

00:05:51.806 --> 00:05:53.433
When I stepped on the court,

00:05:53.474 --> 00:05:55.852
the stars would align,
the world stopped spinning,

00:05:55.894 --> 00:05:58.313
gravity would cease,
and I appeared to jump higher.

00:06:01.107 --> 00:06:03.860
Yeah, I used to be nice.

00:06:05.320 --> 00:06:12.285
(Applause)

00:06:13.286 --> 00:06:17.248
Now I found myself

00:06:18.333 --> 00:06:20.335
becoming obsessed with words

00:06:20.376 --> 00:06:21.920
and how they sounded.

00:06:21.961 --> 00:06:25.381
I started absorbing and paying attention
more to speech patterns

00:06:25.423 --> 00:06:26.716
when people would speak,

00:06:26.758 --> 00:06:28.885
when I was on a train
going through the city.

00:06:28.927 --> 00:06:31.721
I also fell in love with jazz,
because I realized

00:06:31.763 --> 00:06:34.098
that the style
that I was creating for myself

00:06:34.140 --> 00:06:35.600
had elements of improvisation,

00:06:35.642 --> 00:06:36.935
like jazz greats:

00:06:36.976 --> 00:06:40.897
Gil Scott-Heron, Miles Davis, Thelonious.

00:06:40.897 --> 00:06:44.442
I also went and discovered
new poets that I didn’t know.

00:06:44.442 --> 00:06:47.403
That's when I discovered
Langston Hughes and Jean Toomer.

00:06:48.112 --> 00:06:49.405
Now like every story,

00:06:51.074 --> 00:06:53.034
everyone has a love interest.

00:06:53.826 --> 00:06:55.203
And mine was Nikki Giovanni.

00:06:56.079 --> 00:06:58.665
And I actually fell in love with her.

00:07:01.960 --> 00:07:05.213
I found an Afrocentric descended angel ...

00:07:07.256 --> 00:07:08.591
sitting by herself.

00:07:09.926 --> 00:07:11.803
And she is beautiful.

00:07:11.844 --> 00:07:13.805
Certain moments are precious, I was told.

00:07:13.846 --> 00:07:16.724
And when you get to see
into someone's soul, you see heaven.

00:07:16.766 --> 00:07:19.102
So if that's true, heaven is where I am.

00:07:19.143 --> 00:07:22.605
She painted her finger across the sky
like she was painting the stars.

00:07:23.564 --> 00:07:25.483
And the world fell silent as I listened

00:07:25.483 --> 00:07:28.236
to the internal instrumental
that my heartbeat made.

00:07:28.736 --> 00:07:31.781
I walked up behind her
and touched her shoulder like the wind.

00:07:31.781 --> 00:07:35.076
And she looked at me like that familiar
image she sees in her dreams.

00:07:38.830 --> 00:07:43.001
She is like the perfect use of assonance
in just the right amount of lines.

00:07:43.042 --> 00:07:44.293
(Laughter)

00:07:45.086 --> 00:07:49.674
Her pupils looked lost, and I wanted
to teach her to love what she sees.

00:07:49.716 --> 00:07:52.802
Through pictures I have pieced together
her figure like a mosaic,

00:07:52.802 --> 00:07:54.637
and I have her on the walls of my mind

00:07:54.679 --> 00:07:57.807
like graffiti on the cathedrals
of the ghetto called apartments.

00:07:57.849 --> 00:07:59.100
I sat down with her.

00:08:00.226 --> 00:08:01.728
And I grabbed my Moon easel.

00:08:02.937 --> 00:08:04.188
And cloud palette.

00:08:05.398 --> 00:08:07.942
And I started to add stars
to her masterpiece.

00:08:10.528 --> 00:08:14.157
I found an Afrocentric descended angel
that reminded me of Aphrodite,

00:08:14.157 --> 00:08:16.325
and I wondered
what would be her aphrodisiac.

00:08:16.784 --> 00:08:18.161
(Laughter)

00:08:19.746 --> 00:08:21.039
She was sitting by herself.

00:08:22.081 --> 00:08:23.583
And now I sit with her.

00:08:24.459 --> 00:08:26.044
And she is ...

00:08:26.085 --> 00:08:27.670
so ...

00:08:27.712 --> 00:08:28.963
beautiful.

00:08:32.008 --> 00:08:38.848
(Applause)

00:08:39.891 --> 00:08:41.350
Years would pass.

00:08:41.392 --> 00:08:45.688
And unfortunately,
I started to lose a lot of my friends

00:08:45.688 --> 00:08:48.024
to violence and/or jail.

00:08:49.484 --> 00:08:53.988
And it was hard for me not to feel
a sense of survivor's remorse.

00:08:55.073 --> 00:08:56.324
So ...

00:08:56.824 --> 00:09:00.161
it would keep me up at night
on how could I honor them.

00:09:00.161 --> 00:09:03.039
And I realized that I can honor them

00:09:03.081 --> 00:09:05.291
by not only telling my stories

00:09:05.333 --> 00:09:07.043
but telling their stories as well.

00:09:07.627 --> 00:09:10.421
So they could live on the page.

00:09:11.881 --> 00:09:13.174
Forever.

00:09:16.594 --> 00:09:20.515
I’m from a place
with there’s big houses and spouses.

00:09:22.517 --> 00:09:26.104
No, I'm really from a place where
single moms commute two hours to work

00:09:26.145 --> 00:09:29.357
in pencil skirts and nice blouses.

00:09:29.357 --> 00:09:32.110
I'm from a place where dudes
stand in front of the bodega,

00:09:32.110 --> 00:09:33.486
trying to hold their jeans.

00:09:33.486 --> 00:09:35.947
I'm from a place
where there's hustlers and fiends.

00:09:35.947 --> 00:09:37.907
Cops may intervene, some unseen.

00:09:37.907 --> 00:09:40.493
I'm from a place where boys raise boys,

00:09:40.493 --> 00:09:42.995
men are nonexistent and mothers are young.

00:09:43.704 --> 00:09:45.414
So how y'all expect us to turn out?

00:09:47.333 --> 00:09:49.961
Looking up to hustlers was our worth.

00:09:50.002 --> 00:09:52.421
We were told that our awards were skirts.

00:09:53.840 --> 00:09:55.967
See in the hood, fingers get contorted,

00:09:56.008 --> 00:09:58.928
rhetoric retorted,
shots ring out by orphans.

00:09:58.970 --> 00:10:01.013
Now RIP shirts are worn
in front of coffins

00:10:01.055 --> 00:10:02.598
and this happens all too often

00:10:02.640 --> 00:10:05.518
because we’re taught to do that
with no regret, rep your set.

00:10:05.518 --> 00:10:07.812
We’re throwing up signs,
and we ain’t even deaf.

00:10:07.812 --> 00:10:10.940
Kin to death. It's like our lives
are as long as that Bible verse:

00:10:10.940 --> 00:10:12.191
"Jesus wept."

00:10:12.608 --> 00:10:16.737
See, that’s why I can’t wait
till the day when my skin isn’t a novelty.

00:10:16.737 --> 00:10:19.031
And our stories aren’t
synonymous with poverty.

00:10:19.031 --> 00:10:21.242
Because honestly,
it’s hard to deal in modesty

00:10:21.242 --> 00:10:23.619
when we have to deal
with this identity dichotomy.

00:10:23.619 --> 00:10:26.038
Like, why do I have to say I'm human, too?

00:10:26.080 --> 00:10:28.749
When DNA proves I'm deep buried in you.

00:10:28.791 --> 00:10:31.627
We made the pyramids
so you could see a beautiful view.

00:10:31.669 --> 00:10:34.672
We made statues with wide noses.
We were just making the truth.

00:10:34.714 --> 00:10:37.758
So imagine someone saying it wasn't you.

00:10:37.800 --> 00:10:41.637
And whoever did those great things
must have been much lighter than you.

00:10:41.679 --> 00:10:44.348
But how, when you discovered the tombs,

00:10:44.390 --> 00:10:46.559
mummy's hair was even
woven down to the root?

00:10:47.226 --> 00:10:50.062
Truth. So if you really want
to help us young brothers out,

00:10:50.062 --> 00:10:52.690
stop making our schools look like prisons.

00:10:52.690 --> 00:10:55.234
Stop determining how many jails
you're going to build

00:10:55.234 --> 00:10:57.028
off our third grade reading scores.

00:10:57.028 --> 00:10:58.321
Scores, false cause.

00:10:58.321 --> 00:11:01.908
Give Black boy ball and watch him score.

00:11:02.742 --> 00:11:05.411
Give Black boy good beat,
then put him on tour.

00:11:05.411 --> 00:11:08.539
Give Black boy big chain
because he loves the law.

00:11:08.581 --> 00:11:11.292
The crowd just screams
because he never bores.

00:11:11.709 --> 00:11:13.669
But then watch Black people get confused

00:11:13.711 --> 00:11:16.214
because a couple of Black boys
are up in "Forbes."

00:11:16.255 --> 00:11:17.715
Five mic in “Source.”

00:11:17.757 --> 00:11:19.592
Then be biased and make him law.

00:11:19.634 --> 00:11:22.678
Send Black boy to jail,
feed him well because he’s a Moor.

00:11:22.720 --> 00:11:24.847
Now he’s locked down.
Now who miss his amore.

00:11:24.847 --> 00:11:26.515
Now he gets angry. He wants more.

00:11:26.557 --> 00:11:29.352
He screams at the top of his lungs.
His voice gets hoarse.

00:11:29.393 --> 00:11:32.521
Because he remembers when he used to
rock polo with man on horse.

00:11:32.521 --> 00:11:34.982
But of course, this is not
what Black boys cause.

00:11:35.608 --> 00:11:37.860
He just stares, his look is coarse.

00:11:37.860 --> 00:11:41.364
They made his God look nothing like him,
but made him say "Lord."

00:11:41.364 --> 00:11:43.950
Black boy fights
in yard as guards watching us

00:11:43.950 --> 00:11:47.453
like Ali and Foreman all over again
with the same broken jaw.

00:11:47.453 --> 00:11:51.207
Now he saw society clips his wings,
then asks him to soar.

00:11:51.249 --> 00:11:54.085
Now another Black boy dead,
but nobody saw.

00:11:55.419 --> 00:11:58.297
Now another Black mother
crying in the streets, emotions raw.

00:11:58.339 --> 00:12:00.299
Now she prays to that same God, saying,

00:12:00.341 --> 00:12:03.052
"Why, Lord, why did you have to take
my little Black boy?

00:12:03.052 --> 00:12:04.637
He had so much in store,

00:12:04.679 --> 00:12:08.724
but now he's just another young,
dead Black boy."

00:12:10.851 --> 00:12:13.104
You see, I'm from a place
where boys raise boys,

00:12:13.145 --> 00:12:15.147
men are nonexistent and mothers are young.

00:12:16.065 --> 00:12:17.692
So I pray for the Black boy.

00:12:20.361 --> 00:12:27.326
(Applause)

00:12:29.203 --> 00:12:30.454
As you see,

00:12:31.455 --> 00:12:33.457
that little boy that has dyslexia,

00:12:34.917 --> 00:12:37.253
that's spent half
of his educational journey

00:12:37.295 --> 00:12:40.006
in special education and remedial classes,

00:12:40.047 --> 00:12:42.383
that always struggled with English

00:12:42.425 --> 00:12:43.759
and writing

00:12:43.801 --> 00:12:46.971
eventually found his way academically

00:12:47.013 --> 00:12:49.098
and actually started to thrive.

00:12:49.140 --> 00:12:51.851
And that same little boy
made it to Broadway.

00:12:53.102 --> 00:12:54.729
To the mountaintop of writing.

00:12:55.646 --> 00:12:58.858
See, by telling stories
and being authentic to who I am,

00:12:58.858 --> 00:13:02.153
I became the superhero
I wanted to be as a kid.

00:13:02.153 --> 00:13:03.529
Thank you.

00:13:03.529 --> 00:13:05.573
(Applause)

